    {
      "id": "ES-C351-cien",
      "sequence": 6470,
      "title": "cien — a hundred, and the hundred hiding in it",
      "headword": "cien",
      "romanization": "cien",
      "gloss": "a hundred — Latin centum, cut short before a noun; English cent, century and percent keep the Latin, and English hundred is the very same ancient word",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:3543acb040199ee3",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say a different one, then the kind. (Distinto; el tipo.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "veinte — the biggest number you had until now."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "mucho, muy, malo, mal — the same tail-cutting at work."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "SIEN, one beat. The c before i is soft, and ie is a single glide. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cien — \"a hundred.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The full word is ciento, and Spanish cuts the tail off before a noun: cien libros, a hundred books. That is the same trick you have already met with mucho going to muy and malo going to mal."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin centum is the source, and it turns up all over English: a cent is a hundredth, a century is a hundred years, a centipede is a hundred-foot, a centurion commanded a hundred men, and percent is by the hundred."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now the good part. English hundred is the same ancient word. The line that became Latin kept the sound at the front as a c; the line that became English turned that c into an h, all the way down the vocabulary — which is also why Latin cornu matches English horn and Latin cord- matches heart."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So cien and hundred are one word, separated by a single sound change, several thousand years ago."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A number bigger than your fingers, and a rule for shortening it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "cien",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *cien*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "cien libros",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *cien libros*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "cien días",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *cien días*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is the full form of cien? (Ciento.) And which plain English number is the same ancient word? (Hundred.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C351-cantidad",
      "sequence": 6480,
      "title": "la cantidad — the amount, from a question word",
      "headword": "la cantidad",
      "romanization": "la cantidad",
      "gloss": "the quantity, the amount — Latin quantitas from quantus, how much; English quantity, quantum and quantify all ask the same question",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:9aaecaa92b621277",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the kind, then a hundred. (El tipo; cien.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "mucho — a rough answer to the question this asks."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "poco — the other rough answer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "kan-ti-DAD, three beats with the weight on the last. The final d is very soft, close to a breath. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la cantidad — \"the quantity,\" \"the amount.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin quantus is a question: how much? Make a noun of it with -tātem and you get quantitās, how-much-ness — the answer treated as a thing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish has done this to you already. That -tātem is the -dad on the end of verdad, ciudad and edad. Once you spot it, you can hear a Latin abstract noun coming a long way off."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English took the same word twice. Quantity through French, worn smooth. And quantum straight out of Latin, meaning how much there is of a thing — which is why the smallest indivisible amount of energy got that name. Quantify and quantitative follow."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Cantidad is the neutral word. It carries no judgement about whether the amount is big or small; that is what mucho and poco are for."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A noun for the answer to how much."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la cantidad",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la cantidad*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "una cantidad grande",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *una cantidad grande*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la cantidad de libros",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la cantidad de libros*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does Latin quantus ask? (How much.) And which Spanish ending is Latin -tātem? (-dad, as in verdad and ciudad.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C351-ambos",
      "sequence": 6490,
      "title": "ambos — both, and the two sides of ambiguous",
      "headword": "ambos",
      "romanization": "ambos",
      "gloss": "both — Latin ambo, the two together; English ambidextrous, ambiguous and amphitheatre all carry the same both",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:f2f626a87a83233f",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say a hundred, then the amount. (Cien; la cantidad.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "uno, dos, tres, cuatro, cinco — the two this gathers."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "unos, unas — the vaguer handful."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "AM-bos, two beats with the weight on the first. The b after m is a full b, not the soft one between vowels. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ambos — \"both.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish can say los dos. Ambos is the tighter word: the two of them, taken together as a pair rather than counted one after the other."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin ambō is that pair, and the amb- at its front is an old piece meaning on both sides, round about."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English is full of it and rarely notices. Ambidextrous is having a right hand on both sides. Ambiguous is a phrase that drives both ways at once — the -ig- is agere, to drive. Ambition was going round on both sides of a crowd asking for votes. Ambient is what goes round about you."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Greek had the same piece as amphí, which gives amphitheatre, a theatre with seats on both sides, and an amphibian, which lives both lives."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ambos agrees like any adjective: ambos libros, ambas casas."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A word for two things held as one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "ambos",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *ambos*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "ambos libros",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *ambos libros*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "ambas casas",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *ambas casas*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does the piece amb- mean? (On both sides, round about.) And what is an amphibian, word for word? (A thing that lives both lives.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C351-varios",
      "sequence": 6500,
      "title": "varios — several, and the speckled hide behind it",
      "headword": "varios",
      "romanization": "varios",
      "gloss": "several — Latin varius, speckled, of mixed colours; English various, variety and variegated all start from a spotted hide",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:3a53708652a3ba80",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the amount, then both. (La cantidad; ambos.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "poco — fewer than this."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "bastante — more than this."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "BA-rios, with a first syllable va- made with both lips, and the weight on it. -rios is one glide. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "varios — \"several,\" \"a few of different sorts.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin varius did not mean several. It meant speckled — a hide with blotches on it, a sky of broken cloud, marble with veins running through."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "From mottled came mixed, from mixed came of more than one kind, and only then came the plain plural sense Spanish uses now. So varios libros is not merely some books; underneath, it is books that are not all the same."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English kept every step. Variegated leaves are the speckled ones, right back at the start. Variety is a mixture. To vary is to become mottled with change. Various and variance finish the family."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You will hear it in front of its noun, agreeing with it: varias casas."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four counting words, and each one a different way of not saying a number."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "varios",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *varios*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "varios libros",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *varios libros*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "varias casas",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *varias casas*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did Latin varius mean first? (Speckled, blotched.) And which English word for leaves keeps that sense? (Variegated.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C351-triple",
      "sequence": 6510,
      "title": "el triple — three times over, and the fold in simple",
      "headword": "el triple",
      "romanization": "el triple",
      "gloss": "triple, three times as much — Latin triplus, three plus the -plus that means fold; English triple, treble and triplicate are all three folds",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:d61ff94f7beb16df",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say both, then several. (Ambos; varios.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "uno, dos, tres, cuatro, cinco — the three inside it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "mucho — the vague version of the same idea."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "TRI-ple, two beats with the weight on the first. Tr and pl each run together as one sound. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el triple — \"triple,\" \"three times as much.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin triplus is two pieces. Tres is three, which you already count with. The -plus on the end is an old root meaning fold."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So triple is threefold — a cloth creased three times, and therefore three layers thick where it was one."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That fold runs through English in disguise. Simple is one-fold, a thing with a single layer. Double is two-fold. Multiple is many-fold. Triplicate is three copies, three folds of the same paper."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish uses el triple as a noun, with de: el triple de libros, three times as many books."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five ways to count past your fingers: a hundred, an amount, both, several and three times over."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el triple",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el triple*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el triple de libros",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el triple de libros*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "tres o el triple",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *tres o el triple*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does the piece -plus mean? (Fold.) And what is simple, word for word? (One-fold, a single layer.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
